WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:01.590
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk</v>

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.420
about load balancers on AWS.

3
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.120
So you have four kind of managed load balancers.

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.180
You have the Classic Load Balancer, which is old generation,

5
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.600
which supports HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, and SSL,

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.580
which is secure TCP.

7
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.560
You have the ALB, Application Load Balancer,

8
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.330
which is V2, new generation,

9
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.380
and it supports HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket.

10
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.620
You have the Network Load Balancer, also newer generation,

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.364
which supports TCP, TLS,

12
00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:30.390
which is secure TCP, as well as UDP.

13
00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.270
And then you have the Gateway Load Balancer from 2020,

14
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.150
GWLB, which operates at a lower level.

15
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.490
So layer 3, which is network level,

16
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.110
the IP protocol.

17
00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.103
Overall, it's going to be recommended

18
00:00:41.103 --> 00:00:43.980
to use the newer kind of generation load balancers

19
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.237
versus the Classic Load Balancer as they have more feature,

20
00:00:46.237 --> 00:00:48.556
and they're going to be supported by AWS over time.

21
00:00:48.556 --> 00:00:50.068
We're going to do a deep dive on all of those

22
00:00:50.068 --> 00:00:51.600
in this lecture.

23
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.060
Finally, some load balancers can be set up internally

24
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.570
as private or externally as public ELBs.

25
00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.579
So the first one is the Classic Load Balancer,

26
00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:00.973
and you have health checks on it.

27
00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:03.420
They can be at the layer 7 with HTTP

28
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.295
or at the layer 4 with TCP, including based on SSL.

29
00:01:07.295 --> 00:01:09.058
It supports only one SSL certificate,

30
00:01:09.058 --> 00:01:11.276
which is a problem, but the SSL certificate

31
00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:13.710
can have many subject alternative names.

32
00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.590
So SAN, but in that case,

33
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.030
if you want to add or remove a SAN,

34
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.828
then you need to update the SSL certificate

35
00:01:19.828 --> 00:01:22.440
with all the subject alternate name.

36
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.390
It's not ideal, and therefore,

37
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.624
that's why it's recommended to use the newer ALB,

38
00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:28.950
which has SNI, server name indication,

39
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.990
if possible, because you're going to be able to use

40
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.740
multiple SSL certificates on an Application Load Balancer.

41
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.434
And if you want to have distinct SSL certificate,

42
00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:38.760
then from an architectural perspective,

43
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.730
you must use multiple Classic Load Balancer.

44
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.520
In case you're doing TCP to TCP traffic,

45
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.680
then all the traffic is going to go directly

46
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.730
into the EC2 instance.

47
00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.060
It's going to be transiting through the CLB.

48
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.560
And this is the only way you can do

49
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.160
two-way SSL authentication on a Classic Load Balancer.

50
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.940
So the SSL authentication happens at the EC2 level.

51
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.790
So to summarize, the client talks to the CLB,

52
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.000
which talks internally to the EC2 instance.

53
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.173
Next, we have the ALB.

54
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.260
So it's layer 7, only HTTP,

55
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.140
and you can load balance to cross multiple machines

56
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.595
in a target group.

57
00:02:14.595 --> 00:02:17.010
Now, the load balancing can also happen

58
00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.350
to multiple containers on the same machine,

59
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.560
which makes it a great fit to be using with the ECS service,

60
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.150
which leverages the dynamic port-mapping feature of the ALB.

61
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.910
It also has support for HTTP version 2

62
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.070
as well as the WebSocket protocol.

63
00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.974
And you can have some sort of rules and redirects.

64
00:02:33.974 --> 00:02:38.130
For example, you can redirect from HTTP to HTTPS directly

65
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.230
at the Application Load Balancer level,

66
00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.570
but you can also write your own routing rules

67
00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.930
such as path, headers, query strings, and so on.

68
00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:46.980
So for an example,

69
00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.920
here we have an external Application Load Balancer V2

70
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.028
and two target groups, one for the users that is accessible

71
00:02:53.028 --> 00:02:56.640
at the route/user, and the other one for search,

72
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.218
which is available at the route/search.

73
00:02:59.218 --> 00:03:01.140
And as you can see, each target group

74
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.510
will have a different health check.

75
00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.450
Okay, so what are the targets of an ALB?

76
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.013
Their target groups, and it could be either EC2 instances,

77
00:03:09.013 --> 00:03:11.250
and these EC2 instances can be managed

78
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.326
by an Auto Scaling Group.

79
00:03:12.326 --> 00:03:14.160
This is a very common pattern.

80
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.002
It could be ECS tasks managed by ECS itself.

81
00:03:17.002 --> 00:03:18.335
Again, using HTTP.

82
00:03:18.335 --> 00:03:20.490
Or it could be Lambda functions

83
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:24.360
in which case your HTTP request is going to be translated

84
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.183
for your Lambda function into a JSON event.

85
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.090
Then it could also be IP addresses.

86
00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.940
In this case, you must register private IP addresses

87
00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.190
as targets of your ALB.

88
00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.940
Your ALB can also route to multiple target groups at a time,

89
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.070
and you have health checks, as I said,

90
00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.933
at the target group level.

91
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.776
Next we have the Network Load Balancer.

92
00:03:46.776 --> 00:03:50.603
It's layer 4 and allows you to forward TCP

93
00:03:50.603 --> 00:03:53.145
and UDP traffic to your instances

94
00:03:53.145 --> 00:03:56.010
and also to handle millions of requests per second.

95
00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.570
So it's very high performance.

96
00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.771
It has less latency than the ALB,

97
00:03:59.771 --> 00:04:03.600
around 100 milliseconds versus 400 milliseconds for the ALB.

98
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.804
And with the NLB, you're going to get one static IP

99
00:04:06.804 --> 00:04:10.514
per availability zones, and it supports assigning Elastic IP

100
00:04:10.514 --> 00:04:14.340
as the public IP if your NLB is public.

101
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.518
So as a use case, NLB are going to be extremely helpful

102
00:04:17.518 --> 00:04:20.403
when you need extreme performance such as for TCP

103
00:04:20.403 --> 00:04:23.820
or UDP traffic, and the NLB is not included

104
00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.830
in the AWS feature.

105
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.680
For target groups for the NLB, you get EC2 instances.

106
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.080
So they get registered directly

107
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.330
with the NLB and you're good to go.

108
00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.800
It could be IP addresses.

109
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.633
So in case you have, for example,

110
00:04:35.633 --> 00:04:39.150
some on-premises servers you want to register by,

111
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.790
or if you wanted to register your EC2 instances

112
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.220
with the private IP, you could do this as well.

113
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.079
Or finally, a target of your NLB

114
00:04:47.079 --> 00:04:50.250
could be an Application Load Balancer.

115
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.320
And the use case for this would be to leverage

116
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.320
the features of the ALB with, for example,

117
00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.320
layer 7 routing, but you wanna keep the static IP features

118
00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:01.950
of your Network Load Balancers.

119
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.180
So you would chain them together.

120
00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.530
It's a very common pattern.

121
00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.580
Now, another one is around zonal DNS name.

122
00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.210
So if you resolve what's called the regional NLB DNS name,

123
00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.450
which is the DNS name you would use by default,

124
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.211
then it's going to return all the IPs

125
00:05:17.211 --> 00:05:21.030
for all the NLB nodes in all the enabled AZs.

126
00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.700
So this is your NLB DNS name.

127
00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.450
And so if we have the NLB here enabled in three AZ

128
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.530
and we do a DNS query for this NLB DNS name,

129
00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.410
then we're going to get three IP as a return,

130
00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.584
which will correspond to the three AZ that are enabled.

131
00:05:37.584 --> 00:05:42.270
But sometimes you may want to resolve the zonal DNS name.

132
00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.457
And the idea is that the NLB has DNS name

133
00:05:44.457 --> 00:05:48.600
for each of their node, so based on the availability zone.

134
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.843
And using this zonal DNS name, we can resolve

135
00:05:50.843 --> 00:05:54.810
only one IP address, which is the IP address of each node.

136
00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.670
So for example, if you wanted to know

137
00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.480
what was the IP address of the node in US East 1A,

138
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.690
then you would add US East 1A to your DNS name,

139
00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.440
and then it would return only the first IP.

140
00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.430
So why would you do this?

141
00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.620
Why would you want to resolve a specific node?

142
00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:11.790
Well, this could be, for example,

143
00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.490
to minimize latency or data transfer costs.

144
00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.130
For example, say you have an application,

145
00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:21.597
and it's deployed on your VPC in three different AZ.

146
00:06:21.597 --> 00:06:24.750
And it's going to then talk to an NLB.

147
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.300
Then you absolutely want, for example,

148
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.390
your instances in US East 1A to talk

149
00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.547
to the NLB node in US East 1A

150
00:06:32.547 --> 00:06:36.150
because there is going to be no data transfer cost

151
00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.620
and lower latency.

152
00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.062
So in that case, you would resolve the zonal DNS name

153
00:06:40.062 --> 00:06:43.440
for your NLB and only find the one IP

154
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.080
that will ensure you don't have any working cost.

155
00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.270
Because if you use the regional NLB name,

156
00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.250
then there's a chance for you that your application

157
00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.310
sends traffic from one AZ to the other one,

158
00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.260
therefore incurring some cost.

159
00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.611
So to make sure your application sends traffic

160
00:06:57.611 --> 00:07:00.363
to the right NLB node, you need to implement

161
00:07:00.363 --> 00:07:02.430
application specific logic.

162
00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.790
Your application needs to understand in which AZ

163
00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:08.280
it is operating from and then how to query

164
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.057
the right DNS name for your NLB.

165
00:07:11.269 --> 00:07:15.750
The last kind of load balancer is a Gateway Load Balancer.

166
00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.413
It's used to deploy, scale, and manage

167
00:07:17.413 --> 00:07:22.230
a fleet of third-party network virtual appliances in AWS.

168
00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.361
So the use case for a Gateway Load Balancer

169
00:07:24.361 --> 00:07:27.043
is to do firewall, to do intrusion detection

170
00:07:27.043 --> 00:07:28.770
and prevention systems,

171
00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.260
to do deep packet inspection systems,

172
00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.210
or payload manipulation.

173
00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.790
It operates at the network layer, so layer 3,

174
00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.640
which operates directly on the IP packets.

175
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.290
And it combines the following functions.

176
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.000
It's a transparent network gateway.

177
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.950
That means it's a single point of entry and exit

178
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.114
for all traffic, and also it's a load balancer,

179
00:07:46.114 --> 00:07:48.900
because behind the scenes, it can deploy

180
00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.350
and distribute the traffic to your virtual appliances.

181
00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.660
As a technical thing, it uses a GENEVE protocol

182
00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:57.120
on port 6081.

183
00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.668
So a diagram will make it super helpful.

184
00:07:58.668 --> 00:08:01.620
So your users wants to access your application,

185
00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:02.910
which is a destination,

186
00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:04.950
but you want to scan all the traffic

187
00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.220
using third-party security virtual appliances.

188
00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.180
So how do you do?

189
00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.130
Well, you deploy a Gateway Load Balancer,

190
00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.110
and you edit your route tables.

191
00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.670
Now all the traffic is going to go

192
00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.920
through your Gateway Load Balancer.

193
00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.780
The target group of the Gateway Load Balancer

194
00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.360
is going to be these third-party virtual appliances,

195
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.250
and they're going to receive all the traffic,

196
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.320
analyze it, and if it's good,

197
00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.863
keep on sending it back to the Gateway Load Balancer,

198
00:08:27.863 --> 00:08:29.937
which is going to forward that traffic

199
00:08:29.937 --> 00:08:32.400
back to your application.

200
00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.653
So here, the added benefit

201
00:08:33.653 --> 00:08:35.940
is that all the traffic is gonna go

202
00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.720
through your third-party security virtual appliances.

203
00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.390
So the target groups for your Gateway Load Balancer

204
00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.870
could be EC 2 instances, or it could be IP addresses,

205
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.180
and it must be private IPs.

206
00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.131
So that's it for an overview

207
00:08:49.131 --> 00:08:52.260
of all the load balancers on AWS.

208
00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.260
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

